title the repair that reached one caller, and the class nobody had counted
prompt Kyri as Dream: read KYRI and GAUGE_STYLE, honor the ITINERARY inner loop -- roster cold then hot, reds first, QA on touch, the council rota, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first -- pick up the running thread from the newest recommend line, and work the higher Glow rune ports lane under the standing adaptation grant.
think The lap opened on wreckage rather than on work. git status held eighteen staged paths and no commit, which is the exact lap_unclosed state the card names, and HEAD's own subject already read "an unclosed lap" -- the same fault twice in a row. A cold roster refuses to open over a dirty index, so the honest first move was to close the prior lap rather than to route around it: one signed commit of work that was complete in every way except the commit. ls-remote first, measured rather than assumed: xy and debrided both at 9f54393418, my HEAD's parent, so nothing was missed by the pull I then had no need to run.
think The roster cold took 1,676 seconds -- 27m56s -- and I timed it this time, since the prior log recorded that it had not. My first attempt to time it used /usr/bin/time, which this NixOS host does not carry; the run exited 127 having done nothing, and I caught it by reading the timing file rather than the roster's. 70 of 71 green, tree_moved=no. The one red was remember_git_nib, and its cause is the unclosed lap itself: the card's nib is pinned by an amend that follows a commit, so a lap ending at git add leaves the pin naming a round two generations back. That is the fourth firing of that fault (%188, %220, %223) and it books no row, because booking one incident twice is precisely what %287 measured.
think Reds-first over my own lane. Of the six rows in the pin, %230, %252, %255 and %281 are gated at Keaton's door and %273 closed itself by reconstruction, which left %282 -- OPEN, ungated, and described as "the fourth open on BindFailed at rebind." Reading it for its root rather than its symptom is what the fire row asks: %282 had moved one module's sockaddr_in to std.c.sockaddr.in and stopped there, while the same file still spelled SOL_SOCKET as 1, SO_REUSEADDR as 2 and SO_RCVTIMEO as 20. Those are Linux's numbers. Read from the vendored toolchain's own tables rather than from memory, std.c gives a BSD kernel 0xffff, 0x0004 and 0x1006, so setsockopt(fd, 1, 2, ...) names no level that exists there, fails, and -- its return discarded with an underscore -- leaves SO_REUSEADDR unset. An unset SO_REUSEADDR is a rebind that refuses, which is the symptom exactly.
think Then the census, which is where the row actually lives. git grep over the 1,891 authored .rye sources outside vendor, aurora, gratitude and dated testimony found twenty modules carrying forty-six literal socket-option declarations, and nineteen still hand-rolling the Linux-layout sockaddr_in against one dispatched. Five distinct option names appear and every one is Linux's: SO_BROADCAST reads 0x0020 on BSD rather than 6, and SO_BINDTODEVICE does not exist there at all. So %282's repair reached one module of twenty for the struct and zero of twenty for the numbers. That is %281's own lesson one room over -- the fix reached one caller rather than the mechanism.
think The depth of the cut was the real decision, and the fire row names it: exactly as deep as the truth requires and no deeper. Sweeping all twenty is mechanically identical and provably behavior-neutral on Linux, and that is the trap -- this pier cannot test nineteen of them, and a fix closes on a witness rather than on a claim. So the named module is repaired and the class is measured. A ratchet under a ceiling that only falls turns on touch and books nothing, which is the tree's own word for something not yet uniform.
obs I let the guard teach me twice. The tree gates xargs -a and -d at zero and publishes shell_portable.sh for exactly this, so the scan sources xargs_lines rather than spelling a GNU flag; and the fold recital's own paragraph warns that a shelf filename must carry rows- plural or the glob REDS-*rows-*.md reads straight past it. I had written row-288 and renamed it before it shipped. A warning read on the lap it was needed is worth more than one written on the lap it was learned.
obs Two flaws in my own drafts, caught by reading them back rather than by running them. The scan carried a self-exclusion for its own path that filters nothing, since the corpus is git ls-files '*.rye' and the scan is a .sh -- a line that does nothing reads as a line that does something. And the control planted SO_OPT1, which my own pattern cannot match, because [A-Z_]+ holds no digits; a control that plants a shape the scan cannot see proves the ceiling from neither side. Both removed before the first run.
obs The refusal is proven on metal as well as in the pen. One planted literal on this tree took the reading from 19 files and 43 lines to 20 and 44, the scan refused by name and printed the ten worst files, and removing the plant returned it to green. %286's lesson is that a guard proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from a bypass, and the pen alone is the passing direction of the pen.
obs What I could not close, said plainly. caravan_subscribe_poll_signal is RED on this pier at its SIGTERM assertion, and A/B on clean trees -- original module, then repaired, with leftover processes reaped between runs -- produced byte-identical failures, so the repair neither caused nor cured it. It also stands OUTSIDE caravan_suite, which the roster runs, so the choir's green never covered it. That is %282's fourth leg seen from the Linux side and it wants its own lap. %282 therefore stays OPEN with its root now named rather than being marked closed by a pier that cannot see the symptom.
obs Three false trails cost real time and each was worth the cost. A build reported FileBusy and I nearly read it as my change breaking the module; ps showed three of my own leftover poll processes still holding the binary. pkill -f matched my own shell's command line and killed the shell twice, which is why the cleanup now lives in a file rather than in the caller. And pgrep -x refused names longer than fifteen characters, so a process count of two was my own bash line both times -- ps -eo pid,args read zero. Every one of those was a wrong answer that looked like a right one, which is the shape of this whole round.
obs Council rota N mod 5 = 2 -- Fire, Mars, cut and stop, whose sense is vision. It read as the lap's own instruction three times. The cardinal seat is Standfast, and the lap opened by stopping a line that was already stopped and closing it. The fixed seat is reds-first, and the allocation went to %282's root before it went to my own named lane. The dual seat is the three depths of removal, whose lesson is depth perception -- and the whole decision of this round was how deep to cut: one module repaired, nineteen counted, none swept blind.
obs The card fought its bound and that is worth recording rather than hiding. My first Now block ran 3,309 bytes against an old block of 2,422 and carried the card 1,590 over its 24,576. Eight passes of trimming brought it to 24,563 with thirteen bytes of room. The lesson is the card's own: it is a carry card, and the shelf and the log are where detail belongs. I noticed on the way that the REDS pin stands at 24,573 of 24,576 with every row in it OPEN and four gated at Keaton's word -- so the ledger has nowhere flat to put the next open red, which is named on the card rather than repaired, since the bound is a seated number (%205).
obs Grade-what-you-touch, all at Meter: the new shelf, scan, control, witness and the repaired module each read 100/100/100, composite A+. construction/ITINERARY.md read Truth 80 on one unresolved path -- this log, cited before it existed -- and reads whole once it lands. No frame pushed. The module carries nine non-ASCII characters and they stay: all nine are em-dashes inside print() strings, which is program content the ASCII-first rule exempts by name, and the tree's own ascii_comment guard is green on it. My grep was blunter than the rule.
obs The standing adaptation grant went unspent again -- no bash, sh, HTML or Python stood in the way, and the two new fixtures are shell because the guards they join are shell and a portability meter written in one host's dialect proves nothing about the other. Custody gates were not approached: no key, no funds, no chain, no publish, no history rewrite, no collaborator surface.
